{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X1FqssSDiThd2Vee773kAU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"worst\" tier scenario. Let me identify all planned experiments:\n\n1. exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (planned)\n2. exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality pairwise (planned)\n3. exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across judges (planned)\n4. exp-005: Pairwise RATE control (planned)\n5. exp-006: Scaled replication n=300-500 (planned)\n6. exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control (planned)\n\n\n7. exp-013: Human NLI validation (planned)\n\nI'm noticing a discrepancy with exp-013 — the experiment_plan marks it as planned, but the registry shows it's already completed with a negative conclusion and kappa of 0.4304. I should rely on the registry status since it reflects what's actually been done, even though the plan still lists it as pending. I need to identify the planned experiments from the experiment_plan and construct worst-tier predictions for each one. The planned experiments are exp-001, exp-002, exp-003, exp-005, exp-006, exp-009, and exp-013. I'm using the worst tier characteristics as a baseline—only one significant cell across the GPT-4o × LLMBar × formality interaction, non-GPT-4o judges showing win rates between 48-55% with p-values above 0.15, null additional benchmarks, C2 gap replication rates from 0.11-0.44, and specific ranges for binarized Likert and verbosity metrics—to generate consistent predictions for each experiment.\n\nFor exp-001 specifically, I'm looking at multi-judge formality pairwise comparisons. GPT-4o shows a strong formality preference with a 69.1% win rate and p=0.006, which aligns with its training data bias toward formal outputs. Claude-3.5-Sonnet and Gemini-2.0-Flash, however, don't exhibit this same bias because their RLHF training used different data distributions and annotation guidelines, so they fall into the null range with win rates around 51-53% and higher p-values.\n\nFor exp-002, I'm testing whether the formality effect holds across different benchmarks. AlpacaEval and MT-Bench/Arena-Hard both fail to replicate the effect because they have longer, more complex outputs with more confounding variables that degrade counterfactual quality. LLMBar's short, binary-choice format is what makes clean counterfactual generation possible.\n\nFor exp-003, the C2 gap only replicates when using GPT-4o as the judge on LLMBar with pairwise comparisons. Other judge-benchmark combinations show null results in both Likert and pairwise formats, suggesting the effect is highly specific to this particular setup.\n\nFor exp-005, I'm finding that judges consistently prefer the original text over rewritten versions regardless of formality, which indicates a genuine confound from coherence loss that's independent of style effects.\n\nFor exp-006, scaling up to 500 samples shows the GPT-4o × LLMBar formality effect attenuates to a win rate of 0.58 with p=0.02, suggesting the original n=100 result contained some noise, though the effect remains statistically significant at the larger sample size.\n\nFor exp-009, using Llama as the rewriter instead of Qwen produces comparable NLI pass rates and null results for non-GPT-4o judges, confirming that the pattern isn't an artifact of the specific rewriter model but rather reflects which judges have formality bias.\n\nFor exp-013, human NLI validation shows moderate agreement (kappa=0.43) with 91% overall agreement but a concerning 67% false negative rate.\n\nWait, I'm noticing that exp-013 is already completed in the registry with these exact metrics, and the worst tier definition was actually written after observing these results. So this isn't a prediction—it's observed data that matches the worst tier characterization. I should include it in the writeup but clarify that the rationale is about how to interpret these findings.\n\nNow I need to format all of this as YAML.\n\nLooking at the specific numbers across experiments: exp-001 shows both models near chance with win rates around 0.50-0.52 and high p-values indicating no significance. For exp-002, I'm seeing variation across different benchmark and model combinations—AlpacaEval ranges from 0.48 to 0.54 win rate with p-values from 0.22 to 0.62, while MT-Bench shows similar patterns with GPT-4o at 0.56 and Claude at 0.51.\n\nIn exp-003, only one cell in the GPT-4o × LLMBar pairing shows statistical significance (Pairwise p=0.006), while everything else is null, giving a replication rate of just 0.11. But exp-005 reveals a consistent pattern across all judges—GPT-4o, Claude, and Gemini all show significant original preference effects with win rates between 0.59-0.62 and p-values under 0.03, suggesting a systematic confound rather than model-specific behavior.\n\nExp-006 with a larger sample (n=500) confirms the formality effect is real but weaker than initially observed, dropping from 0.69 to 0.59 win rate. Switching to Llama-3.1-70B as the rewriter in exp-009 produces similar results to Qwen, ruling out that as an artifact, though the non-GPT-4o judges still show null effects. The annotation metrics from exp-013 show moderate agreement (kappa=0.43) with very high false negatives (0.667), which the reviewer interprets as evidence against the hypothesis.\n\nThe NLI gate appears to be an unreliable signal for filtering.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17544,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17544},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"928aa605-2770-450b-911e-426134a9106b","uuid":"c9be